<PageLayout type="article" enOnly published="2026-10-23" modified="2026-11-03" title="Weighted vs Unweighted GPA: What's the Difference?" description="An unweighted GPA tops out at 4.0. A weighted GPA adds points for Honors and AP. See worked examples, which one colleges use, and why you can't convert exactly." path="/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa/" heading="Weighted vs Unweighted GPA">
  <p>An unweighted GPA treats every class the same and tops out at 4.0. A weighted GPA adds bonus points for harder classes such as Honors and AP, so it can go higher. Many high schools report both, and knowing the difference helps you read your own record the way a college does.</p>

  <h2>What is an unweighted GPA?</h2>
  <p>An unweighted GPA ignores how hard a class is. An A is 4.0 whether it is in Regular, Honors or AP. The scale tops out at 4.0, so it shows how consistently you earned top grades.</p>

  <h2>What is a weighted GPA?</h2>
  <p>A weighted GPA adds bonus points for advanced classes. Typical rules are +0.5 for Honors and +1.0 for AP, IB and Dual Enrollment, so the scale commonly runs to 5.0 (some schools go higher). It rewards taking harder courses.</p>

  <h2>Side-by-side example</h2>
  <p>A student earns an A in AP Calculus, a B in Honors English and an A in Regular Art (all 1 credit).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unweighted:</strong> (4.0 + 3.0 + 4.0) ÷ 3 = 3.67</li>
    <li><strong>Weighted:</strong> (5.0 + 3.5 + 4.0) ÷ 3 = 4.17</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Can you convert a weighted GPA to unweighted?</h2>
  <p>Not exactly, because a weighted GPA does not show how many advanced classes are behind it. Two students with five 1-credit classes can both have a 4.60 weighted GPA:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Four AP A's and one Regular B: 3.80 unweighted.</li>
    <li>Three AP A's and two AP B's: 3.60 unweighted.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Shortcuts like multiplying by 4/5 would give both of them a 3.68, which is wrong for both. To get the real number, recalculate from your grades without bonuses, or enter your classes in the <a href="/#calculator">calculator</a>.</p>

  <h2>Can a weighted GPA be lower than unweighted?</h2>
  <p>Not when bonuses are added the usual way. Weighted GPA is equal to unweighted if you have no advanced classes, and higher if you have at least one with a passing grade. If your weighted number is lower, your school probably uses a different scale for each, so ask your counselor.</p>

  <h2>Which one do colleges use?</h2>
  <p>Practices vary. The <a href="https://bigfuture.collegeboard.org/plan-for-college/college-basics/how-to-convert-gpa-4.0-scale" rel="noopener">College Board notes</a> that colleges may recalculate GPA, removing noncore classes or adjusting the weighting for AP and honors classes. They can also see your courses, so rigor shows up either way. Scholarships and class rank may use either number, so check each program's rules.</p>

  <h2>Which should you track?</h2>
  <p>Track both. The unweighted GPA shows performance on a common scale, and the weighted GPA shows the benefit of rigor. Our <a href="/#calculator">high school GPA calculator</a> shows both at once, per year and cumulative.</p>

